WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.538
Lecture 11: quicksort.

00:00:02.538 --> 00:00:05.119
Here are 10 numbers to sort.

00:00:05.119 --> 00:00:08.476
So far we have two sorting algorithms

00:00:08.476 --> 00:00:12.348
insertion sort, quadratic in the worst case

00:00:12.348 --> 00:00:14.500
and merge sort, O(n log n).

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.573
Quicksort, invented by Tony Hoare in

00:00:17.649 --> 00:00:20.826
1959, is the third big one.

00:00:20.901 --> 00:00:24.002
It is the fastest known sorting algorithm

00:00:24.078 --> 00:00:26.272
in practice for general data.

00:00:26.347 --> 00:00:28.616
Java's Arrays.sort for int

00:00:28.692 --> 00:00:30.810
arrays uses a version of it.

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:34.601
Its expected running time is O(n log n).

00:00:34.601 --> 00:00:36.977
Its worst case is n²

00:00:36.977 --> 00:00:40.091
but if the pivot is chosen at random

00:00:40.091 --> 00:00:43.204
the worst case is extremely unlikely.

00:00:43.204 --> 00:00:45.089
To make sense of that

00:00:45.089 --> 00:00:48.120
we first need randomized algorithms.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.747
Every algorithm we have met

00:00:50.823 --> 00:00:52.643
so far is deterministic.

00:00:52.643 --> 00:00:54.691
It involves no randomness

00:00:54.691 --> 00:00:56.359
given the same input

00:00:56.359 --> 00:00:59.089
it runs in the same way every time

00:00:59.089 --> 00:01:02.501
so its cost, the number of basic operations

00:01:02.501 --> 00:01:04.700
is also the same every time.

00:01:05.050 --> 00:01:07.612
A randomized algorithm may use

00:01:07.697 --> 00:01:11.455
one more basic operation: RANDOM(x, y)

00:01:11.455 --> 00:01:14.700
which returns an integer from x to y

00:01:14.700 --> 00:01:17.091
chosen uniformly at random.

00:01:17.177 --> 00:01:19.739
Each of the y - x + 1

00:01:19.824 --> 00:01:22.130
integers is equally likely.

00:01:22.480 --> 00:01:24.137
Given the same input

00:01:24.216 --> 00:01:26.268
a randomized algorithm may

00:01:26.347 --> 00:01:28.083
run in different ways

00:01:28.083 --> 00:01:30.845
because its random choices differ.

00:01:30.845 --> 00:01:34.160
So its running cost is a random variable.

00:01:34.740 --> 00:01:36.998
A tiny example: flipCoin.

00:01:36.998 --> 00:01:39.863
It draws RANDOM(0, 1)

00:01:39.863 --> 00:01:42.728
and while the result is not 1

00:01:42.728 --> 00:01:44.118
it draws again.

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:46.722
It keeps flipping a fair coin

00:01:46.809 --> 00:01:48.980
until the coin shows 1.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:53.476
One run might go like this: 0, 0

00:01:53.476 --> 00:01:54.645
0, 1.

00:01:54.645 --> 00:01:56.027
Four rounds.

00:01:56.027 --> 00:02:00.386
Counting two basic operations per round

00:02:00.386 --> 00:02:05.170
one draw and one test, this run costs 8.

00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:09.324
Here are the costs of 10 real runs

00:02:09.324 --> 00:02:13.656
from the Java version in the notes:

00:02:13.656 --> 00:02:18.200
2, 2, 2, 8, 2, 2, 2, 4,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:18.728
6, 2.

00:02:18.834 --> 00:02:21.898
Each run may take a different

00:02:22.004 --> 00:02:23.800
number of rounds.

00:02:24.150 --> 00:02:25.895
What is the worst case?

00:02:25.895 --> 00:02:28.475
The coin could show zero forever

00:02:28.475 --> 00:02:30.676
so flipCoin may never stop

00:02:30.676 --> 00:02:33.863
although the probability of that is zero.

00:02:33.863 --> 00:02:37.278
The worst case tells us nothing useful here.

00:02:37.354 --> 00:02:40.238
For randomized algorithms we study the

00:02:40.313 --> 00:02:42.590
expected running cost instead.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:47.132
Let X be the running cost on a given input.

00:02:47.132 --> 00:02:50.357
The expected running cost, E[X]

00:02:50.357 --> 00:02:54.504
is the average of X weighted by probability

00:02:54.504 --> 00:02:58.650
the sum of each value times its probability.

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:02.559
For flipCoin, the first draw is 1 with

00:03:02.646 --> 00:03:05.337
probability 1/2: cost 2.

00:03:05.424 --> 00:03:08.375
Two rounds happen with probability

00:03:08.462 --> 00:03:10.459
1/4: cost 4.

00:03:10.459 --> 00:03:13.584
Three rounds, 1/8: cost 6.

00:03:13.584 --> 00:03:17.403
In general, a cost of 2i has probability

00:03:17.490 --> 00:03:19.400
1/2^i.

00:03:19.750 --> 00:03:22.499
So E[X] is the sum of

00:03:22.601 --> 00:03:24.637
2i / 2^i.

00:03:24.637 --> 00:03:28.608
The sum of i / 2^i is 2

00:03:28.608 --> 00:03:33.190
so the expected cost is 2 * 2, 4.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:35.430
A constant: O(1).

00:03:36.010 --> 00:03:38.819
Let us check this by simulation.

00:03:38.819 --> 00:03:42.418
In Java, rng.nextInt(2) plays

00:03:42.506 --> 00:03:45.314
the role of RANDOM(0, 1)

00:03:45.314 --> 00:03:48.650
and every round adds 2 to the cost.

00:03:49.000 --> 00:03:50.929
Single runs vary, as we saw.

00:03:50.998 --> 00:03:53.410
But the average over 1 million runs

00:03:53.479 --> 00:03:55.408
is 4.001

00:03:55.408 --> 00:03:57.200
just as the formula says.

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.134
Now a die-rolling version

00:04:00.224 --> 00:04:02.850
keep rolling a fair six-sided

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:05.022
die until it shows 6

00:04:05.022 --> 00:04:08.734
with the same cost model, 2 per round.

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:11.178
What is the expected cost?

00:04:11.178 --> 00:04:13.260
Pause and work it out.

00:04:16.840 --> 00:04:20.153
Each round succeeds with probability

00:04:20.245 --> 00:04:21.994
p = 1/6

00:04:22.086 --> 00:04:24.386
so the expected number of

00:04:24.478 --> 00:04:26.871
rounds is 1/p, 6.

00:04:26.871 --> 00:04:30.920
The expected cost is 2 * 6: 12.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:35.322
For the coin, p is 1/2: 2 rounds

00:04:35.322 --> 00:04:37.550
cost 4, as before.

00:04:38.130 --> 00:04:41.190
Quicksort is divide-and-conquer.

00:04:41.286 --> 00:04:43.485
Assume for now that the

00:04:43.581 --> 00:04:45.685
elements are distinct.

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:49.415
Take 13, 81, 92

00:04:49.415 --> 00:04:52.953
43, 31, 65,

00:04:52.953 --> 00:04:57.257
57, 26, 75, 0.

00:04:57.257 --> 00:05:00.508
Step one: pick a pivot at random.

00:05:00.508 --> 00:05:03.090
Suppose we get 65.

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.022
Step two, partition

00:05:05.101 --> 00:05:07.711
rearrange the elements into those

00:05:07.790 --> 00:05:11.111
smaller than 65, the pivot itself

00:05:11.111 --> 00:05:13.800
and those larger than 65.

00:05:14.150 --> 00:05:16.946
Step three: sort the left part and the

00:05:17.019 --> 00:05:19.741
right part the same way, recursively.

00:05:19.741 --> 00:05:22.389
And then the whole array is sorted.

00:05:22.389 --> 00:05:24.523
There is nothing to combine

00:05:24.596 --> 00:05:26.435
everything on the left is

00:05:26.509 --> 00:05:28.201
smaller than the pivot

00:05:28.201 --> 00:05:31.070
and everything on the right is larger.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.184
Compare merge sort: there

00:05:33.251 --> 00:05:35.151
splitting is trivial and all

00:05:35.218 --> 00:05:36.778
the work is in merging.

00:05:36.778 --> 00:05:39.763
Quicksort puts all the work into the split.

00:05:39.763 --> 00:05:42.476
If values repeat, the idea still works

00:05:42.476 --> 00:05:45.053
equal elements may go to either side

00:05:45.053 --> 00:05:47.020
and only the details change.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.360
Here is the recursion.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:52.000
quicksort(arr, left, right)

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.240
sorts the part of the array

00:05:54.320 --> 00:05:55.840
from left to right.

00:05:55.840 --> 00:05:58.000
If left is at least right

00:05:58.000 --> 00:06:00.800
the range has zero or one element

00:06:00.800 --> 00:06:03.440
which is already sorted: return.

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:07.334
Otherwise, pick the index of a random pivot

00:06:07.334 --> 00:06:09.428
RANDOM(left, right).

00:06:09.428 --> 00:06:11.925
Partition the range around it

00:06:11.925 --> 00:06:15.468
partition returns the pivot's new position

00:06:15.468 --> 00:06:15.968
p.

00:06:16.060 --> 00:06:18.533
Then sort the two subproblems

00:06:18.533 --> 00:06:20.263
left to p minus one

00:06:20.263 --> 00:06:22.324
and p plus one to right.

00:06:22.324 --> 00:06:25.620
The pivot itself is in neither of them.

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:29.194
So the key step is partition.

00:06:29.194 --> 00:06:33.736
It gets the array, the range left to right

00:06:33.736 --> 00:06:38.278
and the index of the randomly chosen pivot.

00:06:38.278 --> 00:06:42.614
For example, 4, 2, 3, 6, 9,

00:06:42.614 --> 00:06:45.814
5, 7, from 0 to 6

00:06:45.814 --> 00:06:49.840
with pivot index 3: the value 6.

00:06:50.190 --> 00:06:54.020
Partition rearranges the range into 4,

00:06:54.020 --> 00:06:57.756
2, 3, 5, then 6, then 7,

00:06:57.756 --> 00:07:01.585
9: smaller values to the left of 6,

00:07:01.585 --> 00:07:04.014
larger ones to its right.

00:07:04.014 --> 00:07:07.750
It returns 4, the pivot's new index.

00:07:08.100 --> 00:07:10.734
And index 4 is exactly where 6

00:07:10.809 --> 00:07:12.916
belongs in the sorted array.

00:07:12.916 --> 00:07:14.195
After partition

00:07:14.195 --> 00:07:16.678
the pivot is in its final place

00:07:16.678 --> 00:07:18.860
and it is never moved again.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:20.386
To sum up.

00:07:20.386 --> 00:07:23.413
One: a randomized algorithm may

00:07:23.507 --> 00:07:25.683
call RANDOM(x, y)

00:07:25.683 --> 00:07:28.899
so its cost is a random variable

00:07:28.899 --> 00:07:32.115
and we measure its expected cost

00:07:32.115 --> 00:07:34.858
for flipCoin, that is 4.

00:07:34.858 --> 00:07:38.831
Two: quicksort picks a pivot, partitions

00:07:38.831 --> 00:07:41.479
and recurses on both sides

00:07:41.479 --> 00:07:43.560
with no combine step.

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:44.390
Three

00:07:44.390 --> 00:07:47.910
partition returns the pivot's new position

00:07:47.910 --> 00:07:49.990
which is its final place.

00:07:50.340 --> 00:07:52.451
Try this before the next part

00:07:52.451 --> 00:07:54.773
during a whole run of quicksort

00:07:54.843 --> 00:07:56.954
how many times can one element

00:07:57.024 --> 00:07:58.502
be chosen as a pivot?

00:07:58.572 --> 00:08:00.472
Think about where the pivot

00:08:00.543 --> 00:08:02.020
goes after partition.

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:04.328
Next, we open up partition

00:08:04.328 --> 00:08:06.938
the Lomuto partition, step by step

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.113
and then the whole randomized

00:08:09.185 --> 00:08:10.490
quicksort in Java.

